detailedExplanation.whatItIs:
A mapped type iterates over keys to build a new type:
type MyPartial<T> = { [K in keyof T]?: T[K] };
type Stringify<T> = { [K in keyof T]: string };
The [K in keyof T] part loops over each property key of T.

A conditional type branches on assignability, and infer extracts a type:
type ElementType<T> = T extends (infer U)[] ? U : T;
type Return<T> = T extends (...args: any[]) => infer R ? R : never;
ElementType<number[]> is number; Return extracts a function's return type.

Variance is about substitutability of composed types:
// covariant: Dog[] assignable to Animal[]
// contravariant params: (a: Animal) => void assignable to (a: Dog) => void
The direction of assignability depends on where the type appears.

Declaration files describe types without code:
// math.d.ts
export declare function add(a: number, b: number): number;
Module augmentation extends existing modules:
declare module 'express' { interface Request { user?: User } }
This adds a user property to Express's Request type.

These are compilerOptions in tsconfig.json:
{ "compilerOptions": { "strict": true } }
Enabling strict implicitly enables strictNullChecks, noImplicitAny, strictFunctionTypes, strictPropertyInitialization, strictBindCallApply, alwaysStrict, and useUnknownInCatchVariables.

Type-level programming computes types from types:
type Length<T extends any[]> = T['length'];
type Reverse<T extends any[]> = T extends [infer H, ...infer R] ? [...Reverse<R>, H] : [];
These compute a tuple's length and reverse it entirely in the type system.

A branded type intersects a base type with a unique tag:
type UserId = string & { readonly __brand: 'UserId' };
type OrderId = string & { readonly __brand: 'OrderId' };
Now UserId and OrderId are distinct even though both are strings underneath.

satisfies validates without widening:
const config = {
  port: 8080,
  host: 'localhost'
} satisfies Record<string, string | number>;
config.port stays inferred as number, and the object is checked against the constraint.

An assertion function uses asserts in its return signature:
function assert(condition: unknown, msg?: string): asserts condition {
  if (!condition) throw new Error(msg);
}
function assertIsUser(v: unknown): asserts v is User { /* checks, throws if not */ }
After calling, the compiler narrows accordingly.

Exhaustiveness checking uses never in a default branch:
function area(s: Shape): number {
  switch (s.kind) {
    case 'circle': return Math.PI * s.r ** 2;
    case 'square': return s.size ** 2;
    default: const _exhaustive: never = s; return _exhaustive;
  }
}
If a new Shape kind is added, s in default won't be never and errors.

A recursive type references itself:
type Json = string | number | boolean | null | Json[] | { [k: string]: Json };
type TreeNode<T> = { value: T; children: TreeNode<T>[] };
Both describe arbitrarily nested structures.

Composition combines and layers utility types:
type PartialBy<T, K extends keyof T> = Omit<T, K> & Partial<Pick<T, K>>;
type Mutable<T> = { -readonly [K in keyof T]: T[K] };
PartialBy composes Omit, Pick, and Partial to make only certain keys optional.

A type guard returns a type predicate:
function isUser(v: unknown): v is User {
  return typeof v === 'object' && v !== null && 'id' in v;
}
if (isUser(x)) { /* x is User here */ }
The v is User return type drives the narrowing.

Overloads expose multiple signatures for one function:
function parse(x: string): object;
function parse(x: number): string;
function parse(x: string | number): object | string { /* impl */ }
Callers see the two overloads, not the broad implementation signature.
